WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.100
What does AD CS hold up today?

2
00:00:02.100 --> 00:00:03.463
It isn't one server.

3
00:00:03.463 --> 00:00:07.825
It's a hierarchy, usually an offline
root and enterprise issuing CAs;

4
00:00:07.825 --> 00:00:12.032
a set of forest-wide objects in
the Public Key Services container,

5
00:00:12.032 --> 00:00:17.174
including the templates and the NTAuth
store; and Group Policy autoenrollment,

6
00:00:17.174 --> 00:00:20.212
which runs on domain
members every eight hours.

7
00:00:20.212 --> 00:00:23.759
The inventory has four layers:
the CAs, the templates,

8
00:00:23.759 --> 00:00:27.800
the certificates actually
issued, and the relying parties.

9
00:00:27.800 --> 00:00:31.740
The relying parties set the order
of work, because a new issuer is

10
00:00:31.740 --> 00:00:36.200
useful only once everything that
validates its certificates trusts it.

11
00:00:36.200 --> 00:00:39.888
Then every template and use
case gets one of four verdicts.

12
00:00:39.888 --> 00:00:45.663
MIGRATE, where Cloud PKI or Entra
certificate-based authentication replaces it.

13
00:00:45.663 --> 00:00:49.816
BRIDGE, where both issuers
run because the consumer

14
00:00:49.816 --> 00:00:54.163
depends on AD or a server:
domain controllers, LDAPS, NPS.

15
00:00:54.163 --> 00:00:58.888
RETAIN, where there's no cloud
equivalent, like HSM-held code signing.

16
00:00:58.888 --> 00:01:01.725
And RETIRE, where the
consumer is going away.

17
00:01:01.725 --> 00:01:07.201
The book corrects its source here:
internal web and SQL Server TLS can't move

18
00:01:07.201 --> 00:01:12.650
to Cloud PKI, and S/MIME stays BRIDGE
because its key history has to survive.
